@subsubsection Loading a System
@anchor{#loading-a-system}
The most trivial use of ASDF is by calling
@code{(asdf:load-system :foobar)} to load your library. Then you can use
it. For instance, if it exports a function @code{some-fun} in its
package @code{foobar}, then you will be able to call it with
@code{(foobar:some-fun ...)} or with:

@verbatim
(in-package :foobar)
(some-fun ...)
@end verbatim


@node Testing a System, Designating a System, Loading a System, Trivial Uses of ASDF
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Testing a System
@anchor{#testing-a-system}
To run the tests for a system, you may use:

@verbatim
(asdf:test-system :foobar)
@end verbatim

The convention is that an error SHOULD be signalled if tests are
unsuccessful.


@node Designating a System,  , Testing a System, Trivial Uses of ASDF
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Designating a System
@anchor{#designating-a-system}
Using keywords to name systems is good and well at the REPL. However,
when writing a program, a bootstrap script, or a system definition, you
SHOULD follow the good style of writing names in the canonical way; and
the canonical name of a system is a string, which by convention is in
lower-case (indeed any symbol used as system designator is downcased by
@code{asdf:coerce-name}).

Thus, the proper way to designate a system in a program is with
lower-case strings, as in:

@verbatim
(asdf:load-system "foobar")
(asdf:test-system "foobar")
@end verbatim

(Historical note: MK-DEFSYSTEM and before it Genera's SCT used
string-upcase to normalize system names, and use that as filenames,
which made sense on Genera's filesystem or using logical pathnames as
common with MK-DEFSYSTEM. Dan Barlow's ASDF, to ``Play Nice With Unix'',
preferred lower-case file names, and used string-downcase to normalize
symbols into strings; at the same time, he allowed you to specify mixed
case or upper-case names by using a string. The latter was probably a
mistake, and I strongly recommend against using anything but lower-case.
If that weren't allowed, there would be a case for making lower-case
symbols the default syntax, and let the various case conversions do
their job without quotes. But that is not where we are. In any case,
system names designate @code{.asd} files, not Lisp bindings, and this
determines their syntax.)


@node Trivial System Definition, Trivial Testing Definition, Trivial Uses of ASDF, Trivial Examples
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsection Trivial System Definition
@anchor{#trivial-system-definition}
A trivial system would have a single Lisp file called
@code{foobar.lisp}. That file would depend on some existing libraries,
say @code{alexandria} for general purpose utilities, and @code{trivia}
for pattern-matching. To make this system buildable using ASDF, you
create a system definition file called @code{foobar.asd}, with the
following contents:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar"
  :depends-on ("alexandria" "trivia")
  :components ((:file "foobar")))
@end verbatim

 how the type @code{lisp} of @code{foobar.lisp} is implicit in the
name of the file above. As for contents of that file, they would look
like this:

@verbatim
(defpackage :foobar
  (:use :common-lisp :alexandria :trivia)
  (:export
   #:some-function
   #:another-function
   #:call-with-foobar
   #:with-foobar))

(in-package :foobar)

(defun some-function (...)
  ...)
...
@end verbatim

@menu
* Using the system you defined::
@end menu


@node Using the system you defined,  , Trivial System Definition, Trivial System Definition
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Using the system you defined
@anchor{#using-the-system-you-defined}
Assuming your system is installed under the @code{~/common-lisp/}
hierarchy or some other filesystem hierarchy already configured for
ASDF, you can load it with: @code{(asdf:load-system "foobar")}, or
shorter @code{(asdf:make :foobar)}

If your Lisp was already started when you created that file, you may
have to @code{(asdf:clear-configuration)} to re-process the
configuration.


@node Trivial Testing Definition, Trivial Packaging, Trivial System Definition, Trivial Examples
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsection Trivial Testing Definition
@anchor{#trivial-testing-definition}
Even the most trivial of systems needs some tests, if only because it
will have to be modified eventually, and you want to make sure those
modifications don't break client code. Tests are also a good way to
document expected behavior.

The simplest way to write tests is to have a file
@code{foobar-tests.lisp} and modify the above @code{foobar.asd} as
follows:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar"
  :depends-on ("alexandria" "trivia")
  :components ((:file "foobar"))
  :in-order-to ((test-op (test-op "foobar/tests"))))

(defsystem "foobar/tests"
  :depends-on ("foobar" "fiveam")
  :components ((:file "foobar-tests"))
  :perform (test-op (o c) (symbol-call :fiveam '#:run! :foobar)))
@end verbatim

The @code{:in-order-to} clause in the first system allows you to use
@code{(asdf:test-system :foobar)} which will chain into
@code{foobar/tests}. The @code{:perform} clause in the second system
does the testing itself.

In the test system, @code{fiveam} is the name of a popular test library,
and the content of the @code{perform} method is how to invoke this
library to run the test suite @code{:foobar}. Obviously your mileage may
vary if you use a different library.

@menu
* Note on ASDF 2 compatibility::
@end menu


@node Note on ASDF 2 compatibility,  , Trivial Testing Definition, Trivial Testing Definition
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Note on ASDF 2 compatibility
@anchor{#note-on-asdf-2-compatibility}
The @code{:in-order-to ((test-op (test-op ...)))} idiom will not work
with ASDF 2: attempts to @code{test-system} will result in failure due
to circular dependencies; however, @code{load-system} will still work.
As for the @code{:perform} method, you need to specify @code{:after}
right after @code{test-op} for ASDF 2 to accept the @code{defsystem}
form at all (but still not make the @code{:in-order-to} work); so it's
probably OK if your test system is in a separate @code{.asd} file, but
if you want backward compatibility and it's a secondary system, you'll
use @code{:after}. Making things work completely on ASDF 2 as well as
ASDF 3 would take a lot of pain, and would be colloquial for neither.

But @emph{friends don't let friends use ASDF 2}. ASDF 3 has been
available since 2013, and all implementations have now been providing it
for a long time. Many systems, and growing, assume ASDF 3, possibly some
you depend on, and it's pointless to maintain compatibility with a
moribund system (especially so if you don't also check that all your
dependencies do, too). Upgrade your implementations and/or at least your
ASDF, and tell your friends to do as much. The
@uref{http://fare.livejournal.com/149264.html,very founding principle of
ASDF 2} (and 3) was to provide users with the ability to install a newer
ASDF on top of an old one to fix its bugs (an ability that did not exist
with ASDF 1); thus, users do not have to maintain compatibility with
antique bugs.

These days, I even recommended that you should freely rely on features
of ASDF 3.1 (2014) not present in ASDF 3.0 (2013), such as:
@code{~/common-lisp/} being included in the source-registry by default;
@code{run-program} being full-fledged with input and error-output
support; or the @code{package-inferred-system} feature. Conversely, if
you want indefinite backward compatibility, why stop at ASDF 2? Why not
also support ASDF 1, and MK-DEFSYSTEM, and the original Lisp Machine
DEFSYSTEM? A good rule of thumb for me is that it is not worth writing
workarounds for bugs that were fixed or features that were changed two
years ago or more. Two years seems to be the time it takes for a release
of ASDF to become ubiquitously available by default on all
implementations; and, remember, if a user must use an older
implementation, he can always trivially install a newer ASDF on top of
it. Thus, by October 2019, people should not be shy about dropping
support for ASDF versions older than 3.3. And even before then, if you
need a recent ASDF, just document it, and tell your users to upgrade
their implementation and/or install a recent ASDF on top of their
implementation's.


@node Trivial Packaging,  , Trivial Testing Definition, Trivial Examples
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsection Trivial Packaging
@anchor{#trivial-packaging}
In the previous testing code, @code{symbol-call} is a function defined
in package @code{uiop}. It helps deal with the fact that the package
@code{:fiveam} isn't defined yet at the time the @code{defsystem} form
is read. Thus you can't simply write @code{(fiveam:run! :foobar)}, as
this might cause a failure at read-time when file @code{foobar.asd} is
first read and the system @code{fiveam} isn't loaded yet. This of course
also holds for packages defined by system @code{foobar} itself.

To get a symbol in it that is not a function being called, you can also
use @code{(find-symbol* :some-symbol :foobar)}, and you can use
@code{symbol-value} to get the value bound to such a symbol as denoting
a variable. For complex expressions, you can use @code{eval} and
@code{read-from-string}, as in
@code{(eval (read-from-string "(foobar:some-fun foobar:*some-var*)"))},
or equivalently, also from @code{uiop}:
@code{(eval-input "(foobar:some-fun foobar:*some-var*)")}.

System definition files are loaded with the current @code{*package*}
bound to the package @code{:asdf-user}, that uses the packages
@code{:cl}, @code{:asdf} and @code{:uiop}. Therefore, you don't need to
and you SHOULD NOT specify @code{cl:}, @code{asdf:} or @code{uiop:}
prefixes when referring to symbols in these respective packages. It is
considered bad form to do so, unless you changed the package, and even
then, your package should probably use these packages, too. In
particular you SHOULD NOT to write @code{(asdf:defsystem "foobar" ...)}
instead of @code{(defsystem "foobar" ...)}. Also, you SHOULD NOT use
@code{(in-package ...)} or @code{(defpackage ...)} if all you do can
legitimately be done in package @code{ASDF-USER}.

System definition files are loaded by a special function
@code{asdf::load-asd}; this function locally binds the current
@code{*package*} to the @code{:asdf-user} package, but it also does many
other things. You MUST NOT ever load a @code{.asd} file using
@code{cl:load}, as this will not work in general, and may fail in subtle
or spectacular ways. You MUST always use @code{asdf::load-asd} for that
purpose (the function is exported in ASDF 3.2 and above, but not in
earlier versions). You SHOULD NOT encourage the illusion that a
@code{.asd} file can be loaded with @code{cl:load} by using
@code{(in-package :asdf-user)} or anything, or by using the package
prefix @code{asdf:} for @code{defsystem} and other such symbols. You
SHOULD be using the @code{slime-asdf} extension to SLIME if you are
going to edit @code{.asd} file and then load them from SLIME, as it will
automatically use @code{load-asd} to load the file contents.

