@subsubsection Building a System
@anchor{#building-a-system}
Some systems offer operations that are neither loading in the current
image, nor testing. Whichever operation a system is meant to be used
with, you may use it with:

@verbatim
(asdf:make :foobar)
@end verbatim

This will invoke @code{build-op}, which in turn will depend on the
@code{build-operation} for the system, if defined, or @code{load-op} if
not. Therefore, for usual Lisp systems that want you to load them, the
above will be equivalent to @code{(asdf:load-system :foobar)}, but for
other Lisp systems, e.g.@ one that creates a shell command-line
executable, @code{(asdf:make ...)} will do the Right Thing™, whatever
that Right Thing™ is.


@node Inspecting a System, Other Operations, Building a System, Simple Uses of a System
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Inspecting a System
@anchor{#inspecting-a-system}
To look at what ASDF knows of your system, try:

@verbatim
(describe (asdf:find-system "foobar"))
@end verbatim

If you're looking for the @code{.asd} file, try
@code{(asdf:system-source-file "foobar")}. For the directory in which
the @code{.asd} file resides, try
@code{(asdf:system-source-directory "foobar")}. For a specific file
under that directory try
@code{(asdf:system-relative-pathname "foobar" "path/to/the/file.extension")}.


@node Other Operations,  , Inspecting a System, Simple Uses of a System
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Other Operations
@anchor{#other-operations}
ASDF has the concept of an @emph{operation} that can act upon a system
(or a smaller component thereof). Typical operations that matter to
end-users include:

@itemize
@item
@code{load-op} for loading a system in the current Lisp image, as used
by @code{asdf:load-system},
@item
@code{test-op} for running tests associated to the system, as used by
@code{asdf:test-system},
@item
@code{build-op} for doing whatever build operation is associated to the
system, (which by default is @code{load-op} if the system didn't
override it), as used by @code{asdf:make}.
@end itemize

Further operations of interest to users include:

@itemize
@item
@code{compile-op} (ensure the system is compiled, without necessarily
loading all of it, or any bit of it),
@item
@code{load-source-op} (load the system from source without compiling),
@item
@code{compile-bundle-op} (create a single fasl for the entire system,
for delivery),
@item
@code{monolithic-compile-bundle-op} (create a single fasl for the entire
system @emph{and all its transitive dependencies}, for delivery),
@item
@code{image-op} (create a development image with this system already
loaded, for fast startup),
@item
@code{program-op} (create a standalone application, which we will see
below), etc.
@end itemize

Whichever operation you want, @code{(asdf:operate}@emph{operation}
@emph{system}@code{)} will ensure this operation is performed on that
system (after all the necessary dependencies of such an action). A
short-hand @code{asdf:oos} is available for @code{asdf:operate} (the
name @code{oos} is an old acronym for @code{operate-on-system}, and both
functions existed as synonyms in the old MK-DEFSYSTEM; the latter
function doesn't exist in ASDF, which instead has just @code{operate}).

The operation is typically specified as a symbol that names the
operation class. Since ASDF 3, you can also use a keyword to specify an
action in the ASDF package. Thus, @code{(asdf:oos :load-op :foobar)} is
equivalent to @code{(asdf:load-system :foobar)}.


