@node Primary Systems, Secondary Systems, System Naming, System Naming
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Primary Systems
@anchor{#primary-systems}
ASDF has a notion of @emph{primary system}, that it can find in
configured parts of the filesystem (the @emph{source-registry}), in a
file that has the same name and the type @code{asd}. Thus, primary
system @code{foobar} is defined in a file @code{foobar.asd}.

While arbitrary strings are accepted in system names, it is strongly
discouraged to use anything but lower-case ASCII letters and digits for
primary system names, plus the separators @code{-} (hyphen) and @code{.}
(dot). The @code{.} itself is only recommended in primary system names
that are part of an informal hierarchy; for instance the popular library
@code{iolib} contains many related systems: @code{iolib.asdf},
@code{iolib.base}, @code{iolib.common-lisp}, @code{iolib.conf},
@code{iolib.examples}, @code{iolib.grovel}, @code{iolib.tests}. The main
one is ostensibly @code{iolib}, but it contains many systems, and for
some reasons (notably proper phase separation) they cannot all be
secondary systems in the same file.


@node Secondary Systems,  , Primary Systems, System Naming
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Secondary Systems
@anchor{#secondary-systems}
A @emph{secondary system} is a system defined in the same file as a
primary system. By convention, its name starts the same as the file's
primary system, followed by a slash @code{/} and by a @emph{suffix} made
of some arbitrary characters, preferably ASCII letters and digits plus
the separators @code{-} (hyphen), @code{.} (dot) and @code{/} (slash).
We already saw an example of it with system @code{foobar/tests} in the
trivial testing definition above.

The convention above allows ASDF 3 and later to find a secondary system
by name, by first looking for the associated primary system. ASDF 3.2 or
later will issue a @code{warning} when you violate this naming
convention.

(Historically, ASDF 1 (and 2) allowed arbitrary names for secondary
systems, and in practice many people used @code{foo-test} or such for
secondary system names in @code{foo.asd}; however, ASDF 1 (and 2)
couldn't find those systems by name, and horrific bugs could happen if a
system was simultaneously defined in multiple files.)


@node Simple System Definition, Simple Packaging, System Naming, Simple Examples
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsection Simple System Definition
@anchor{#simple-system-definition}
A simple system may be made of many files.

Typically, in @code{package.lisp} (or @code{packages.lisp}) you'll
define the package (or packages) used by all the files in your system in
one or more forms such as @code{(defpackage :foobar ...)}.

Then, in a package @code{utils.lisp} you'll define utility macros and
functions that you're using throughout your system; maybe some of them
deserve to be moved to @code{alexandria} and other utility systems; and
maybe they already exist out there and you just haven't looked hard
enough.

Then a file @code{foobar.lisp} defines the meat of your system.

Your system definition will look like:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar"
  :depends-on ("alexandria" "trivia")
  :serial t
  :components
  ((:file "package")
   (:file "utils")
   (:file "foobar")))
@end verbatim

The @code{:serial t} indicates that each of these files depends on (all)
the previous.

As your system grows some more, soon enough instead of a single file
@code{foobar.lisp}, you will have several files, one for each aspect of
your system, such as @code{foo.lisp}, @code{bar.lisp} as well as
@code{foobar.lisp}. Moreover, @code{:serial t} soon becomes
inappropriate: it will make your code slower to compile, but also to
read. Indeed, those who read the code won't be readily able to tell
which parts of the code they need to keep active in their brains to
understand the code at hand. Instead, you may prefer to explicitly
represent the dependencies between the components of your system using
@code{:depends-on} clauses as follows:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar"
  :depends-on ("alexandria" "trivia" "trivia.ppcre")
  :components
  ((:file "package")
   (:file "utils" :depends-on ("package"))
   (:file "foo" :depends-on ("utils"))
   (:file "bar" :depends-on ("utils"))
   (:file "foobar" :depends-on ("foo" "bar"))))
@end verbatim

Out of good style, you SHOULD still list the components in an order that
makes sense, such that the readers can read the files and mentally
rebuild the system. However, note that this order, if coherent, will be
respected only in ASDF 3.3 or later, due to a bug in earlier versions of
ASDF. But that precise order shouldn't matter, or it should be reflected
in the @code{:depends-on} declarations (or in a @code{:serial t}
declaration).


