@node Initial Package for a Lisp File, Using Symbols from ASDF and UIOP, Simple Packaging, Simple Packaging
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Initial Package for a Lisp File
@anchor{#initial-package-for-a-lisp-file}
You MAY assume that the current package uses @code{CL} at the beginning
of a file, but you MUST NOT assume that it is any particular package at
this point: right now, it @emph{is} guaranteed to be the @code{CL-USER}
package of the underlying implementation, but it is conceivable that in
some indeterminate future, some extension to CL may provide a
well-defined portable alternative that ASDF would use.

Therefore, the sane way to write a Lisp file is that it SHOULD start
with an @code{in-package} form, optionally preceded by a
@code{defpackage} form (or a @code{uiop:define-package} form). You
SHOULD NOT write @code{cl:in-package} or precede your @code{defpackage}
with an @code{(in-package :cl-user)} (which is stupid, because to be
pedantic you'd have to @code{(cl:in-package :cl-user)}, at which point
you may as well @code{(cl:defpackage ...)} and
@code{(cl:in-package ...)}). If it's a regular @code{cl-source-file}, it
can assume the language is CL indeed, and that the readtable something
reasonable, etc.


@node Using Symbols from ASDF and UIOP,  , Initial Package for a Lisp File, Simple Packaging
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Using Symbols from ASDF and UIOP
@anchor{#using-symbols-from-asdf-and-uiop}
You MAY use any of the symbols documented and exported by ASDF or UIOP.
Actually, it is warmly recommended to use them everywhere that it
matters, instead of less documented or less portable alternatives.

You MUST NOT use @code{asdf:run-shell-command},
@code{asdf:system-definition-pathname}, or other deprecated functions
that were once recommended in the time of ASDF 1. They will be removed
in the near future (one to two year horizon). ASDF 3.2 or 3.3 will issue
a @code{style-warning} when you do, and some future version of ASDF will
issue a full @code{warning}, which will then break the SBCL build. See
@code{backward-interface.lisp} for a list of deprecated function --- or
just heed the damn warnings.


@node Simple Testing, Other secondary systems, Simple Packaging, Simple Examples
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsection Simple Testing
@anchor{#simple-testing}
Test systems can also be divided in multiple files. If possible (which
is not always the case), the file names for test files should match file
names for regular code files, so that the file that tests
@code{bar.lisp} will be called @code{bar-test.lisp} or
@code{bar-tests.lisp} (pick singular or plural, but have a story for it
and be consistent about it). To keep things tidy as the test system
grows, you may even put all test files in a subdirectory @code{t/},
@code{test/} or @code{tests/}. Your test system definition may then look
like:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar/tests"
  :depends-on ("fiveam" "foobar")
  :pathname "t/" ;; specify the subdirectory
  :components
  ((:file "test-suite")
   (:file "utils-test" :depends-on ("test-suite"))
   (:file "foo-test" :depends-on ("test-suite"))
   (:file "bar-test" :depends-on ("test-suite"))
   (:file "foobar-test" :depends-on ("test-suite")))
  :perform (test-op (o c) (symbol-call :foobar/tests :run-test-suite)))
@end verbatim

As the system and its test system both grow, the test system may be
moved to its own file @code{foobar-tests.asd} or @code{foobar.tests.asd}
where it is its own primary system:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar-tests" ...)
@end verbatim


@node Other secondary systems,  , Simple Testing, Simple Examples
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsection Other secondary systems
@anchor{#other-secondary-systems}
Other secondary systems may be created beyond test systems: for instance
systems that provide independent aspects of the system, or optional
add-ons to it. One case is a command that makes the Lisp functionality
accessible from a Unix shell.

@menu
* Delivering an Executable::
* System Connections::
@end menu


@node Delivering an Executable, System Connections, Other secondary systems, Other secondary systems
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Delivering an Executable
@anchor{#delivering-an-executable}
To build an executable, define a system as follows (in this case, it's a
secondary system, but it could also be a primary system). You will be
able to create an executable file @code{foobar-command} by evaluating
@code{(asdf:make :foobar/executable)}:

@verbatim
(defsystem "foobar/executable"
  :build-operation program-op
  :build-pathname "foobar-command" ;; shell name
  :entry-point "foobar::start-foobar" ;; thunk
  :depends-on ("foobar")
  :components ((:file "main")))
@end verbatim

The @code{build-pathname} gives the name of the executable; a
@code{.exe} type will be automatically added on Windows. As a horrible
kluge that may go away in the future, the output will be created in the
system's directory (where the @code{foobar.asd} file resides) when the
@code{build-operation} matches the requested operation. A future version
of ASDF will probably instead have some @code{:output} argument to
@code{operate}, or some such thing, and drop this ugly special case. You
have been warned. Contact me if you want that sooner, or not at all.

There, file @code{main.lisp} defines a function @code{start-foobar} in
package @code{foobar}, that takes no argument, and initializes and
starts the executable --- after @code{uiop} calls its own
@code{*image-restore-hook*} and evaluates any provided
@code{*image-prelude*}. Importantly, libraries may register functions to
call in the @code{*image-restore-hook*} using
@code{register-image-restore-hook} (see also
@code{register-image-dump-hook}); UIOP and ASDF themselves make use of
this facility. Typically, @code{start-foobar} will be defined as
something like:

@verbatim
(defun start-foobar () (main (uiop:command-line-arguments)))
@end verbatim

Where function @code{main} parses the arguments (a list of strings,
excluding the magic C @code{argv[0]}, which can be computed as
@code{(uiop:argv0)}) and does whatever its magic. You may want to use
the full-featured @code{net.didierverna.clon} or my small
@code{command-line-arguments} or one of a slew of other libraries to
parse the command-line arguments. You may want to use
@code{cl-scripting} to nicely wrap Lisp code into execution contexts
that handle errors in a nice(r) user-visible way for the shell user. You
may want to use @code{inferior-shell} if your program in turn invokes
other shell programs.

Instead may also use @code{cl-launch} to build executables, or
@code{buildapp}. @code{cl-launch} is largely compatible with ASDF
(indeed, a lot of code formerly written as part of @code{cl-launch} was
later made part of ASDF 3's UIOP). @code{buildapp}, that came before
ASDF 3 but after @code{cl-launch}, has a slightly incompatible
convention where a main function is called with a list of arguments that
includes the @code{argv[0]}. (@code{cl-launch} and after it UIOP had
many good enough reasons to start the argument list at the ``user
arguments'', if only because when invoking a Lisp implementation via
@code{cl-launch}, the process' @code{argv[0]} may not be available or
meaningful, whereas the user arguments may be only a subset of the
actual process' arguments.) Both @code{cl-launch} and @code{buildapp}
have similar functionality to handle multicall binaries à la
@uref{http://busybox.net/,Busybox}, with the same incompatibility as
above.


@node System Connections,  , Delivering an Executable, Other secondary systems
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection System Connections
@anchor{#system-connections}
Sometimes, a system can provide an extension to another system. For
instance, if you use both @code{metacopy} and @code{contextl}, you may
be interested in some system @code{metacopy/with-contextl} that creates
synergies between these two systems.

There exists a system @code{asdf-system-connections} that will allow you
to define such system connections that are @emph{automatically} loaded
when both the connected systems are loaded. While I debugged that
mechanism and made sure it works with ASDF 3, I recommend against using
it, because it introduces side-effects within the build. Instead I
recommend explicitly loading the system connections as part of the
larger system that will use them.


