@node force, force-not, More Elaborate Uses of ASDF, More Elaborate Uses of ASDF
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection force
@anchor{#force}
Sometimes, you want to force ASDF to re-build some system. At those
times, you can pass the @code{:force} argument to @code{asdf:operate}
(or its wrappers @code{asdf:load-system}, @code{asdf:test-system},
@code{asdf:oos}, etc.). Passing an argument @code{t} will force rebuild
of just the system, and none of its dependencies (and that also means
none of the dependencies that happen to be secondary systems with the
same primary system name). Passing a list of system designators
(preferably lower-case strings) will force the specific systems to be
rebuilt (if they appear in the build plan at all, that is). Finally,
passing @code{:all} as argument will force a rebuild of everything,
including all dependencies.

Thus, if you ran tests that use the @code{prove} test framework, in
which loading the files is itself the test, and you want to force a
re-run, even though ASDF might be satisfied with already having loaded
the files, then you can use:
@code{(asdf:load-system :clack-test :force t)}

Note that it is strongly recommended to not have any non-determinism or
side-effects that are not declared to ASDF, that would cause the forcing
to be meaningful. Forcing is thus a debugging feature for ASDF systems
that fail this good practice. In the case of @code{prove}, we will have
to work with its author so that the correct way to use it doesn't
violate ASDF invariants, but instead properly declare that ASDF should
not consider tests already run.


@node force-not, Require, force, More Elaborate Uses of ASDF
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection force-not
@anchor{#force-not}
The converse of @code{:force} is @code{:force-not}, and you can specify
a list of systems to not rebuild. In this context @code{t} means
``everything but this system'' rather than " this system``.
@code{force-not} takes precedence over @code{force}, and by default
includes a list of''immutable" systems that may be used when delivering
extensible applications to customers (See function
@code{asdf::register-immutable-system}).

Note that these flags are only for use by the user at the toplevel. You
MUST NOT call @code{asdf:operate} with @code{:force} or
@code{:force-not} from within a build. Actually, you should probably not
explicitly use @code{asdf:operate} at all, except maybe inside a
@code{.asd} file in cases where @code{defsystem-depends-on} isn't
sufficient.


@node Require,  , force-not, More Elaborate Uses of ASDF
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Require
@anchor{#require}
You SHOULD NOT use @code{cl:require} as a substitute for
@code{asdf:load-system}. You SHOULD NOT use @code{asdf:require-system}
except at the toplevel.

Back in the days of ASDF 1, a convenient hook was added to ASDF so that
when you call @code{cl:require}, it would try to load the named system,
and if not fall back on the builtin require mechanism. This was a cool
hack, and when you merely wanted a dependency, it was easier to type
@code{(require :foo)} than @code{(asdf:operate 'asdf:load-op :foo)} as
you then had to. Moreover, on SBCL where ASDF was developed, ASDF itself
was used to compile and load SBCL modules at runtime, so this hook came
naturally.

As ASDF maintainer, I now consider this in bad taste:

@itemize
@item
First, this hook is not 100% portable, so it is bad taste to recommend
relying on it.
@item
Second, @code{cl:require} has a mechanism for loading things only once
by checking @code{cl:*modules*} which may subtly interfere with ASDF's
mechanism for keeping things up to date.
@item
Third, it interferes with ASDF's capacity to detect legitimate vs
illegitimate recursive uses of @code{operate} at places that defeat
tracking of dependencies.
@item
Fourth, it adds a lot of complexity for dubious gain: at a time you had
to type @code{(asdf:operate 'asdf:load-op :foo)}, @code{(require :foo)}
may have been a nice short-hand, but it isn't such a great gain over
@code{(asdf:make :foo)}.
@item
Fifth, SBCL now uses ASDF 3's @code{compile-bundle-op} to create a fasl
during the build of SBCL itself, and that fasl can latter be loaded at
runtime without ASDF. Therefore the hook has no natural use anymore.
@end itemize

Similarly, in ASDF 2.21 I added a function @code{require-system} that
used to called @code{load-system} with
@code{:force-not (already-loaded-systems)}, which was a nice hack at the
time, that I latter used as part of the @code{cl:require} hook. That was
all a big mistake, as @code{:force-not} interferes with the ability to
keep a coherent plan across recursive uses of @code{asdf:operate} as
required by builds that involve @code{:defsystem-depends-on} and other
ASDF extensions. These days, this function only checks whether the
requested component is already loaded, and if not calls
@code{asdf:load-system} on it. This function MUST only be used at the
toplevel, never in a script or build. It may be deprecated in a future
version of ASDF.


