@node Source File Types, Conditional Outputs and Conditional Perform, Defining ASDF Extensions, Defining ASDF Extensions
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Source File Types
@anchor{#source-file-types}
You MUST NOT define methods on @code{source-file-type}. This bad ASDF 1
interface must die and will be removed in a future version of ASDF.
Instead, override the slot @code{type} for your file class and provide a
proper @code{:initform}, as in:

@verbatim
(defclass cl-source-file.l (cl-source-file) ((type :initform "l")))
@end verbatim

You can then provide the name of that class as class name for individual
components, or as @code{:default-component-class} to relevant systems or
modules.


@node Conditional Outputs and Conditional Perform,  , Source File Types, Defining ASDF Extensions
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@subsubsection Conditional Outputs and Conditional Perform
@anchor{#conditional-outputs-and-conditional-perform}
An operation MUST actually create all the outputs declared by the
@code{output-files} method. If some of these outputs are conditional,
the @code{output-files} method MUST check the condition and remove
uncreated outputs from the list. If some output is sometimes non
meaningful but that is only known after calling the @code{perform}
method, it is sometimes appropriate to create an empty file instead of
not creating the file.

If you conditionally avoid performing an action altogether using an
@code{:around} method, you MUST still call @code{mark-operation-done} on
the action in the branch of the method where the action is not
performed. In that case, both the @code{output-files} and
@code{input-files} methods should probably return @code{nil} when that
condition is met. Alternatively, if avoiding some actions is uniform for
all operations on a given component, you MAY want to override or wrap
around the @code{component-if-feature} method for that component instead
of defining all those @code{perform}, @code{output-files} and
@code{input-files} methods.


